; joybus_device.loom
; A controller-side device for the single-wire controller bus of the
; Nintendo 64 and GameCube ("joybus"): it listens for the console's command,
; decodes each bit by the length of its low, and after the console stop bit
; answers the info command (0x00 or 0xFF: 3 bytes), the N64 poll (0x01: 4
; bytes) and the GameCube poll (0x40 with its two argument bytes: 8 bytes)
; from a table the host sets, ending each reply with the device stop bit.
;
; Written on 2026-11-05, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035): it is in no design document.
; There is no public specification from the console makers; every figure
; comes from public secondary sources, cited in tools/protomodels/joybus.py.
; The program is a timing device on an open-drain line, nothing more: the
; table's bytes mean whatever the host puts there. It uses:
;   - an open-drain BIDIR pin (OD_MASK), built for I2C;
;   - the timed waits, WAITP with a timeout (SEMANTICS 6.4), built for the
;     receivers' frame timeouts: every console edge has a deadline, and a
;     frame that goes wrong sends the program back to waiting for a quiet
;     line, never into a hang;
;   - the bit engine's SHI (SEMANTICS 6.9) as a one-bit sampler, CNT
;     counting the command bits;
;   - the deadline-latched pin write (SETP ... D, SEMANTICS 6.10), built for
;     ws2812: the reply's edges are exact to the clock, and its first fall
;     a fixed RESP after the console stop bit's rise is seen;
;   - LD/ST (SEMANTICS 6.11), built for device emulation, for the table.
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: the bit decision by low
; time, the command dispatch, the turnaround within 2 us, the three-to-one
; reply shapes and the device stop bit, the resynchronisation.
; Weaker than a dedicated peripheral: a command that is not one of the four
; gets no answer (official devices do the same: the console reports a
; timeout, N64brew), and the table is changed only between frames.
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-06): the console
; makers publish no specification, so every figure is from the public
; secondary sources named above, and four are unchecked: the device's
; reply delay (2 us taken; the only bound is "within about 62.5 us"), the
; device stop bit's 1 us low (joybus-pio only), the GameCube 0x41 reply
; length (so 0x41 is not answered), and any tolerance (the model's
; +-0.25 us windows are its own). Nothing here is game content: it is the
; bus timing and the command set only.
;
; Pin: JB = BIDIR1, open drain (OD_MASK bit 1). Nothing drives the line high.
;
; Host words (FIFO space, thread 1).
;   INQ, two words per table entry: the index I (bits 2:0; the rest
;     ignored), then the value. The table, TAB[0..7], two bytes a word, the
;     first sent in bits 15:8:
;       TAB[0]     info bytes 1, 2: the device identifier (reset 0x0500, an
;                  N64 controller; a GameCube controller is 0x0900)
;       TAB[1]     bits 15:8 info byte 3, the status (reset 0x00); 7:0 unused
;       TAB[2..3]  the N64 poll reply, 4 bytes
;       TAB[2..5]  the GameCube poll reply, 8 bytes (the first 4 are the
;                  N64 ones: one table, one console at a time)
;       TAB[6..7]  unused (read ahead by the sender, never sent)
;     The program reads INQ only while the line is quiet (between frames):
;     an entry pushed during a frame is taken after it. Push both words of
;     an entry together: the second POP waits for its word.
;   OUTQ: not used.
;
; Replies (bytes, MSB first, then the device stop bit):
;     0x00, 0xFF   TAB[0] and the high byte of TAB[1]: 3 bytes
;     0x01         TAB[2..3]: 4 bytes
;     0x40 a b     TAB[2..5]: 8 bytes (a and b are read and ignored; on a
;                  GameCube they set the poll mode and the rumble, int03)
;
; Rate: TICK_INT = TK = 10 clocks, 0.2 us at 50 MHz, as joybus_host.loom.
;
; Timing (us; sources in tools/protomodels/joybus.py):
; Receiving: a frame is taken only after the line has been high QUIET_IN
; (5 us, more than any high inside a frame, 3 us), so a fall in the middle
; of a frame never starts one. Each bit's line is sampled THR = 9 ticks
; after its fall is seen, 1.84 to 2.08 us after the fall on the model (as
; joybus_host.loom measures it).
; After a bad frame (a low or a high too long, or an unknown command) the
; program waits for QUIET (8 us) of high line before it listens again.
; Turnaround: the console stop bit is 1 us low; its rise is seen 2 to 6
; clocks late and the reply's first fall lands RESP = 10 ticks after the
; slot that saw it, about 2.2 us after the rise. The sources give no figure
; for a controller's delay (N64brew: within about 62.5 us); 2 us is the
; "few microseconds" real controllers take, unchecked.
; Sending: as joybus_host.loom, every edge latched: lows of exactly 1 and
; 3 us, cells of exactly 4 us; the device stop bit is 1 us low (joybus-pio;
; N64brew gives only its 4 us length).
;
; OD_MASK (BUGS 14): as joybus_host.loom: set, read back three slots
; later, set again if lost.
;
; Memory: 125 words of code in thread 1's quarter, 0x080..0x0FC, and the
; 8-word table at TAB = 0x1F8, the end of thread 3's quarter (thread 3
; must not be started with this program loaded).
;
; Registers: r0 the command byte, then the sender's 16-bit countdown; r1 the
; reply word on top; r3 the reply bits; r4 the receive phase (1: the 0x40
; arguments), then the next reply word; r5 the table pointer; r6 0x00FF;
; r7 scratch and the `.csr` scratch.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 1
        .equ    JBPIN = 1               ; BIDIR1
        .pins   JB = JBPIN
        .equ    JBBIT = 1 << JBPIN
        .equ    TK = 10                 ; clocks per 0.2 us tick at 50 MHz
        .equ    T1 = 5                  ; 1 us
        .equ    T3 = 15                 ; 3 us
        .equ    QUIET_IN = 25           ; 5 us of high line before a frame
        .equ    LISTEN = 250            ; 50 us: then INQ is looked at again
        .equ    QUIET = 40              ; 8 us after a bad frame
        .equ    THR = 9                 ; the bit's sample, after the fall
        .equ    LOW_TO = 12             ; the rise, by sample + 2.4 us
        .equ    HIGH_TO = 20            ; the next fall, by rise + 4 us
        .equ    STOP_TO = 10            ; the console stop bit's low, 2 us
        .equ    RESP = 10               ; the turnaround, 2 us
        .equ    DIR = 1 << 1            ; BE_CFG: MSB first, no CRC
        .equ    TAB = 0x1F8
        .equ    TAB_INFO = TAB          ; the info reply
        .equ    TAB_POLL = TAB + 2      ; the poll replies

start:  CSRR    r7, OD_MASK
        ORI     r7, JBBIT
        CSRW    OD_MASK, r7             ; JB open drain
        NOP
        NOP
        CSRR    r7, OD_MASK
        ANDI    r7, JBBIT
        BZ      start                   ; lost to the other thread's write
        .csr    TICK_INT, TK            ; one tick = 0.2 us; also the checker's .tick
        LDI     r7, 0
        CSRW    TICK_FRAC, r7
        LDI     r7, DIR
        CSRW    BE_CFG, r7
        LDI     r7, (JBPIN << 5) | JBPIN
        CSRW    BE_PINS, r7             ; SHI samples JB
        LDI     r6, 0xFF
        SETP    JB, 1                   ; let go of the line

; Between frames: the table entries the host has pushed.
idle:   SETD    0
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T               ; TD is reached: done at once
        BT      listen                  ; nothing waiting
        POP     r0                      ; I
        POP     r1                      ; the value (pushed with I)
        ANDI    r0, 7
        MOV16   r7, TAB
        ADD     r7, r7, r0
        ST      r1, r7, 0
        JMP     idle

; A frame starts with a fall after QUIET_IN of high line.
listen: SETD    LISTEN
        WAITP   JB, 1, T
        BT      idle                    ; held low: look at INQ, try again
        SETD    QUIET_IN
        WAITP   JB, 0, T
        BNT     resync                  ; a fall inside a frame
; The line is quiet: it stays so while no fall comes, so a timeout below
; goes back here, past the quiet test, unless INQ has a word.
quiet:  LDI     r7, 8
        CSRW    CNT, r7
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; the command byte first
        SETD    LISTEN
        WAITP   JB, 0, T                ; the command's first fall
        SETD    0                       ; anchored on the fall (if it came)
        BNT     rbit
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T               ; TD is reached: done at once
        BT      quiet                   ; nothing waiting: still quiet
        JMP     idle

; The command, MSB first: each bit begins at a fall, anchored by the SETD 0
; right after the wait that saw it.
rbit:   WAITD   THR
        SHI                             ; the line at fall + 2 us; Z = (CNT == 0)
        BZ      word
rrise:  SETD    LOW_TO                  ; (SETD, WAITP and BT keep Z)
        WAITP   JB, 1, T                ; a 1 has risen, a 0 rises at 3 us
        BT      resync
        SETD    HIGH_TO
        WAITP   JB, 0, T                ; the next bit's fall
        SETD    0                       ; anchored on it (keeps T)
        BNT     rbit
        JMP     resync

; A byte is in (between the sample and the rise): what to answer.
word:   TEST    r4, r4
        BNZ     stop                    ; the arguments of 0x40 are in
        STSR    r0
        AND     r0, r0, r6              ; the command byte
        MOV16   r5, TAB_POLL            ; the poll replies (info: below)
        LDI     r7, 0x40
        CMP     r0, r7
        BNZ     not40
        LDI     r7, 16
        CSRW    CNT, r7                 ; its two argument bytes
        LDI     r4, 1
        LDI     r3, 64
        JMP     rrise
not40:  LDI     r3, 32
        CMPI    r0, 1
        BZ      stop                    ; the N64 poll
        SUBI    r5, 2                   ; TAB_INFO
        LDI     r3, 24
        TEST    r0, r0
        BZ      stop                    ; info
        CMP     r0, r6
        BNZ     resync                  ; not one of the four: no answer

; The console stop bit after the last bit's rise, then the turnaround.
stop:   SETD    LOW_TO
        WAITP   JB, 1, T                ; the last bit's rise
        BT      resync
        SETD    HIGH_TO
        WAITP   JB, 0, T                ; the stop bit's fall
        BT      resync
        SETD    STOP_TO
        WAITP   JB, 1, T                ; ... and its rise: the turnaround
        BT      resync
        SETD    0
        SETP    JB, 0, D
        LD      r1, r5, 0               ; the first reply word ...
        LD      r4, r5, 1               ; ... and the next
        ADDI    r5, 2
        LDI     r0, 16
        WAITD   RESP                    ; the reply's first fall lands

; The reply, MSB first, every edge latched (as joybus_host.loom).
dbit:   SHLI    r1, 1                   ; C = the bit
        SETP    JB, 1, D                ; stage the rise
        BC      d1
        CALL    feed                    ; a 0: in its 3 us low
        WAITD   T3                      ; the rise at fall + 3 us
        SETP    JB, 0, D
        WAITD   T1                      ; the next fall at + 4 us
        DJNZ    r3, dbit
        JMP     dstop
d1:     WAITD   T1                      ; a 1: the rise at fall + 1 us
        SETP    JB, 0, D
        CALL    feed                    ; in its 3 us high
        WAITD   T3                      ; the next fall at + 4 us
        DJNZ    r3, dbit
dstop:  SETP    JB, 1, D                ; the device stop bit: 1 us low
        WAITD   T1

; After a frame, good or bad: QUIET of high line, then listen again.
resync: SETD    QUIET
        WAITP   JB, 0, T
        BT      idle                    ; quiet: back to INQ and listening
        SETD    LISTEN
        WAITP   JB, 1, T                ; wait out the low (bounded)
        BT      idle                    ; held low
        JMP     resync

; feed: shift the next word's top bit into r1; every 16 bits, the next word.
feed:   SHLI    r4, 1
        BNC     fz
        ORI     r1, 1
fz:     DJNZ    r0, fr
        LDI     r0, 16
        LD      r4, r5, 0
        ADDI    r5, 1
fr:     RET

        .org    TAB
tab:    .word   0x0500, 0x0000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0
